I know a natural language, namely Tok Pisin, which has a predicate marker,=
=20
namely "i". Tok Pisin and the other Bislamic languages (which also have "i"=
,=20
though in Solomon Islands Pijin it's often found attached to "hem") are abo=
ut=20
two hundred years old and formed by creolization, which is kids adding=20
grammar to a pidgin that didn't have a complete grammar. So it appears to m=
e=20
that a predicate marker is a kind of word that humans are predisposed to.

I don't know of any natural language that has a terminator for the article.=
=20
Terminators are a part of speech peculiar to the Loglanic languages and are=
=20
there to make the language syntactically unambiguous while being fully=20
expressive.

So I think it's important to teach both "ku" and "cu" early and that there =
are=20
constructions where they may be used but don't have to and others where the=
y=20
have to be there.

Pierre
